# Marquise Cut Hidden Halo Engagement Ring ({{year}}): 4 Design Flaws Buyers Skip

![Three loose marquise cut diamonds held in jeweller tweezers against a soft grey backdrop as the DiamondCritics featured image for the {{year}} marquise cut hidden halo engagement ring guide titled 4 design flaws buyers skip](/images/marquise-hidden-halo-engagement-ring.jpg "Marquise Cut Hidden Halo Engagement Ring 4 Design Flaws Buyers Skip")

## TL;DR — Marquise Hidden Halo Engagement Ring in One Scroll

**A marquise hidden halo engagement ring looks like a solitaire from above and a halo from the side.** That is the whole point. The hidden halo is a ring of accent stones sitting below the centre marquise, invisible when you look straight down but visible when the ring tilts or catches the light. It is modern, understated, and misunderstood. Buyers skip 4 design flaws that cost the entire effect.

**The 4 design flaws buyers skip (all 4 covered in full below):**

- **Flaw 1 — Buying it for the face-up size trick.** A hidden halo does NOT make the stone look bigger from above. If you want the 1.5x visual multiplier, go visible halo instead.
- **Flaw 2 — Thinking the hidden halo protects the tips.** It sits under the stone, not around it. The two sharp marquise points are still exposed. V-tip centre prongs are still mandatory.
- **Flaw 3 — Ignoring the under-stone pavé drop risk.** Hidden halo accents sit in deep micro-prong seats. Over 3–5 years, 10–20% pop out. Budget for free retailer re-set service.
- **Flaw 4 — Pairing hidden halo with a thick band.** The whole point of hidden halo is a low profile. A thick 2.5mm+ shank defeats it. Pick a petite shank or let the halo disappear visually.

