## How to Verify a Marquise Hidden Halo Before You Buy

Three checks every ring must pass before you commit.

**Check 1 — Confirm centre prong spec is V-tip six-prong.** The hidden halo adds zero tip protection. V-tip six-prong at the centre is mandatory on any marquise hidden halo. The retailer will spec this at order for $0 extra on most listings. See [GIA 4Cs](https://4cs.gia.edu/) for centre stone certification standards.

**Check 2 — Watch the 360° video for side-view sparkle quality.** The whole value of hidden halo is the tilt-reveal flash. If the side-view sparkle looks dim or the accent stones blend into the metal, the design is poorly executed. Walk if the side-view does not flash cleanly.

**Check 3 — Confirm the shank width is 1.5–2.5 mm (petite to standard).** A thick 2.5mm+ shank defeats the low-profile hidden halo silhouette. If the shank is wider than 2.5mm, pick a different setting or ask the retailer for a petite-shank variant. [IGI](https://www.igi.org/) and GIA both certify loose stones only — the shank measurement is on the retailer's listing.

I run these three checks before I quote any client on a hidden halo ring. The hidden halo is beautiful when paired with V-tip centre prongs, a clean side-view sparkle execution, and a petite shank. It is a $1,255 wasted investment when any one of those three fails.

